d a little girl are  among those who died french police  arrested four people with suspected  links to the incident  the covet crisis in europe has hit  another dangerous level with multiple  countries setting new records for daily  cases the czech republic hungary the  netherlands and slovakia all reported  their highest number of daily infections  today with cases surging across europe  more countries are imposing tough new  restrictions slovakia is entering a  two-week lockdown in italy 20 small  towns have imposed an 8 pm curfew france  is expected to reveal new measures later  this week  there was a stark warning today about  the escalating crisis in europe the who  says the region is once again the  epicenter of the pandemic last week  europe accounted for more than 60  percent of all reported cases and deaths  worldwide officials say there's a false  sense of security around vaccines and  even vaccinated people must take more  precautions  even in the midst of a very very strong  resurgence in cases we're seeing  pre-pandemic levels of social mixing  gathering and many other things and and  the reality is the virus will continue  to transmit intensely in that  environment  the eu's public health agency is now  recommending booster shops for all  adults in europe to help protect people  from infection  money milking still ahead why you can  expect to dole out more for dairy in the  new year  [Music]  expect the price of dairy to jump next  year the canadian dairy commission is  proposing a record increase for 2022  farmers would be paid more but the cost  of milk cheese and other canadian dairy  products would go up too critics say the  crown corporation is giving more weight  to producers than consumers when setting  prices eric sorenson has more  if you're worried about rising food  costs get ready for a hike in dairy  products producers have been hit by  higher fuel prices and a big jump for  animal feed it's gone up by 27  and and there's really there's no  shortcuts we can take with that cows  have to be fed to produce milk but the  proposed payments to dairy farmers is  contentious the canadian dairy  commission's usual formula initially  determined a price increase of 6.4  percent according to internal documents  obtained by global news the previous  record increase was 4.5 percent but  dairy farmers felt they needed more and  the commission ultimately recommended  8.4 percent almost double the previous  record  it's necessary to keep up with his costs  says one farmer we're playing catch up  because i've had to pay those costs for  the last six months retailers and  restaurants who will pay more for milk  wanted just a 3.3 percent hike critics  believe decision making at the canadian  dairy commission favors producers under  a supply management regime that already  guarantees those farmers a minimum price  this expert says the cdc needs reform  make it more transparent change the  governance of  the cdc which would include processors  restaurant and consumers as well  give them a voice what about the price  of milk the price of milk is nuts indeed  that's why we're working on  affordability the trudeau government  says dairy farmers are not abusing the  system last year  only about nine percent of them had  revenues over  their expenses so i think the work that  the commission has done  is fair we've also been clearer i mean  this government is going to  protect the supply managed sector but  that sector will usher in milk prices up  to 10 percent higher by some estimates  which would hit hard low-income families  already facing other price increases  these very vulnerable families are going  to be drowning  in increased prices fluid milk is a  staple for canadian diet therefore  there's a social safety net that needs  to be put around this important  product the supply management system  does ensure a safe and adequate supply  of milk for canadians but if provincial  authorities approve new dairy prices  will kick in february first eric  sorensen global news toronto  up next dart defense how nasa's latest  test mission might help save planet  earth  take a look at this dramatic footage of  spain's la palma volcano a new stream of  lava has reached the ocean sending  clouds of potentially toxic gases into  the sky as the volcanic material cools  it extends the island's surface it has  already grown by 43 hectares since the  eruption began just over two months ago  well it may sound like a flashback to a  hollywood blockbuster but nasa's latest  mission is very real the space agency  has just launched dart a spacecraft  designed to smash into an asteroid it's  supposed to test if technology can  actually stop dangerous space debris  from hitting our planet so how worried  should we be mike trolley explains  if you gaze towards space long enough  you're bound to see a shooting star and  as people say make a wish three  two  one  well nasa's latest launch is less about  the things we wish upon and more about  anything we wish will avoid earth  altogether but if there's anything  that's slightly larger so anything  bigger than 30 30 meters in size it  could cause some damage depending on  where it impacts anything that's really  really big bigger than 140 meters in  size so just like a small football  stadium it could be devastating for life  on earth and that's where dart comes in  vehicles supersonic it's a small  spacecraft designed to deliberately  crash into an asteroid as part of nasa's  planetary defense strategy  its target is dimorphous a small  asteroid orbiting a larger one and no  neither is on a collision course with  earth nor is any other large asteroid  they're tracking  this is merely a test to see if they can  alter dimorphous's course so if the  earth was ever in peril we could do  something about it that's how seventy  percent of all life on earth died so if  something of significant size hit us  we're gonna go bye-bye it's not gonna be  good for us  a camera will record darts collision  next september which will no doubt be  thrilling i know they are partying it up  uh wherever they're watching from it  just won't be hollywood thrilling  in fact while dart will collide with  dimorphis at 24 000 kilometers per hour  it will be more like a thud than an  explosion  and maybe then the dart team can let  loose  [Applause]  nasa style  microlight global news toronto  and that is global national for this  wednesday night i'm robin gill tonight  your canada is the muscadabit river in  nova scotia we would love to see your  corner of the country so please keep  sending your photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  thank you for watching donna friesen  we'll be back at the anchor desk  tomorrow and i'll see you again this  weekend have a great night  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

